// StdToX64Conversion — Std → Target (x64) lowering. Takes a StdModule and
// produces a fresh TargetModule of x64 `TargetOp`s. Each StdOp is lowered to its
// x64 op sequence; the result still uses VIRTUAL registers (X64VReg.virtual),
// which the register allocator colors to physical GPRs afterward.
//
// This is the ONLY tier boundary below Std — shv2 has no MIR tier (see
// StdDialect's header for why v1's was dropped). The virtual registers this pass
// consumes are Std's own `ValueId`s, which have BEEN SSA virtual registers since
// the Maxon→Std lowering minted them; v1's MIR tier never added a value model,
// only a copy of one.
//
// THIN for maxon-shv2 M1: lowers exactly the `return 42` slice —
//   StdOp.const(id, v, valueType) -> constMaterializationOp (movRegImm32 / movRegImm)
//   StdOp.ret(id)  terminator     -> mov R8, <retval>   + ret
// The primary return value goes to **R8**, not RAX — Maxon's self-designed
// calling convention (v1 does the same). Later milestones GROW the dispatch with
// the ALU / compare-branch / general-call / memory arms + the float (XMM) return
// path as the Std dialect grows. v1's per-function lowering structure (a skeleton
// clone of the block graph, then a per-function op walk) is preserved so the
// per-function fan-out driver (M5) can wrap one function at a time.
//
// NOTE ON FRAMES: unlike v1's ISel — which emits `epilogue` at every return and
// lets the prologue/epilogue pass strip/re-emit it — shv2's return lowering emits
// ONLY the R8 move + `ret`. Frame setup AND teardown are owned entirely by the
// prologue/epilogue pass (InsertPrologueEpilogue), which inserts `prologue` at
// entry and `epilogue` before the return terminator when the function needs a
// frame. This removes the emit-then-strip dance; at M1 no user function needs a
// frame, so main lowers to a flat `mov reg,42; mov r8,reg; ret`.

// The low end of the range the 32-bit immediate form can carry. `mov r32, imm32` ZERO-
// extends into the full 64-bit register, so it spans the UNSIGNED 32-bit domain and
// nothing below it — see constMaterializationOp for what happens when this bound is
// dropped.
let Imm32ZeroExtendedMin = 0 as MachineWord

// THE size choice for materializing an integer constant into a GPR — the single place it
// is made, because two copies of it have to agree BYTE FOR BYTE: the live-range splitter
// REMATERIALIZES a constant at each use, and the op it re-emits must be identical to the
// one this pass emitted (SplitLiveRanges.rematerializeConstant).
//
//   0 <= value <= u32.max -> movRegImm32   the 5-byte `mov r32, imm32`, which ZERO-extends
//                                          its immediate across the whole 64-bit register
//   otherwise             -> movRegImm     the 10-byte `mov r64, imm64`
//
// THE LOWER BOUND IS LOAD-BEARING, and it was missing. `value <= u32.max` alone is true of
// every NEGATIVE value as well (MachineWord is signed), so `-1` took the zero-extending
// form and landed in the register as 0x00000000FFFFFFFF — while a later 64-bit `cmp reg,
// -1`, whose imm32 SIGN-extends, compared it against 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF and found them
// unequal. It stayed latent because no `const` op ever carried a negative value: a source
// `-7` parses as `7` under a `neg`. Type-qualified bounds (`u64.max` = -1, `i64.min`) are
// the first negative constants to reach here.
//
// EVERY form this emits is a `mov`, and `mov r, imm` WRITES NO FLAGS. `StdOp.const` declares
// `clobbersFlags: false` on the strength of exactly that, and compare/branch fusion depends on
// it: a short-circuit's seed literal sits between a compare and its branch, so a `const` that
// claimed to clobber flags cost 3 instructions on every `if a > 0 and b > 0`.
//
// ⚠ SO IF YOU ADD THE value==0 `xor reg, reg` ZEROING IDIOM HERE — the shorter encoding, still
// tempting — YOU MUST FIRST DEAL WITH `StdOp.const.clobbersFlags`. `xor` DOES write flags, so
// `const`'s flag behaviour would become VALUE-DEPENDENT (flag-neutral for every value but 0),
// which per-variant metadata cannot express: split the variant, or take `clobbersFlags: true`
// back and accept the lost fusion. Landing the idiom alone is a SILENT MISCOMPILE — a `jcc`
// would read flags the `xor` had already destroyed. This is the flag-neutral half of the
// "flag-neutral-vs-shorter" tradeoff, and it is not free.
export function constMaterializationOp(dest X64VReg, value MachineWord) returns TargetOp
	if value >= Imm32ZeroExtendedMin and value <= u32.max 'fitsUnsigned32'
		return TargetOp.movRegImm32(dest, value: value)
	end 'fitsUnsigned32'
	return TargetOp.movRegImm(dest, value: value)
end 'constMaterializationOp'

// The custom-ABI integer ARGUMENT registers, in slot order: argument `k` arrives in
// (and is passed in) `argRegisters[k]`. This is v1's order MINUS its 7th slot rbx —
// rbx is CALLEE-saved in shv2, so it must never carry an argument. All six are
// caller-saved and in the allocatable pool. The RETURN value uses R8, which is not an
// argument register, so there is no return/argument register conflict. The array LENGTH
// is the register-argument cap: a 7th argument would need a stack slot (a later
// milestone), which the parser rejects up front (see `MaxRegisterParams`).
let argRegisters = [X64Register.rcx, X64Register.rdx, X64Register.rax, X64Register.r9, X64Register.rsi, X64Register.rdi]

// The physical register argument `index` occupies. An index past the register file is
// a parser-cap violation (parseFunctionParameters rejects >6 parameters), so it is a
// compiler-invariant break here, not a user error.
function argRegisterAt(index ParamCount) returns X64Register
	return try argRegisters.get(index) otherwise panic("argRegisterAt: argument index {index} exceeds the {argRegisters.count()} register-argument slots — the parser caps parameters at that count until stack arguments land")
end 'argRegisterAt'

// Maps the SSA value a FUSED `cmp` defines to the predicate it compared with. Populated
// when a fusable `cmp` is lowered and consumed at the `condBranch` terminating its block —
// the two become `cmp` + `jcc`, and no boolean is written to a register. Keyed by ValueId,
// which is function-local, so the map is reset per function.
//
// A cmp that is NOT fusable is deliberately ABSENT here, which is what makes
// `lowerCondBranch`'s lookup the single source of truth: a hit means "the flags this branch
// reads were set by the compare that defined its condition", and that fact is established at
// exactly one place (`computeFusedCmps`).
typealias CmpPredMap = Map with (ValueId, StdCmpPred)

// A block whose float-equality branch was split → the id of the `ordered` block minted for it.
// See `reserveFloatEqualityBlocks`, which is the only writer, and `lowerCondBranch`, the only
// reader. A MISS means "this block's branch is not a float equality", which `try/otherwise`
// says without a sentinel.
//
// It holds the block's ID and not its ref on purpose: the walk reads this while holding a live
// borrow of `targetModule.blocks` (its `dstBlock`), and recovering an id from a ref would need
// a second borrow of that same array — the thing `moveBranchEdgeToSplit` scopes its helpers
// apart to avoid. An id is a scalar and needs no borrow at all.
typealias OrderedBlockIdMap = Map with (BlockRef, BlockId)

// The immediate a boolean condition is tested against. x64 branches on FLAGS, so a
// condition that is a VALUE (a call's `bool` return, a `param`, a short-circuit merge phi,
// a materialized compare) must first be compared with zero: `cmp reg, 0` + `jne` is "is it
// true?". Named because it is a CONTRACT — the same 0/1 domain `setccReg` and the logical
// `not` (`xor reg, 1`) produce.
let BoolFalseImm = 0 as Imm32

// The mask the LOGICAL `not` flips: a bool lives in the 0/1 domain, so complementing it is
// flipping bit 0 — `xor reg, 1` — and NOT the 64-bit one's complement `not` reg (which
// would turn true into -2, still truthy). The INTEGER `not` is a separate Std opcode, so
// this constant can never be applied to one by accident.
let LogicalNotMask = 1 as Imm32

// The two FIXED GPRs a materialized float equality folds its answer through — see
// `materializeFloatEquality` for why the fold cannot touch a virtual register at all until its
// single publishing `mov`.
//
// NOTHING pins these two in particular: any pair of distinct caller-saved GPRs would serve, so
// they are named here rather than picked at the use site. RCX and RDX are the pair `lowerShiftCl`
// and `lowerDivMod` already reach for, which keeps the set of registers this backend pins to
// fixed roles as small as possible.
//
// Writing them is a PHYSICAL DEF, which is what makes them safe: liveness forbids each to every
// value live ACROSS that write, so the fold cannot destroy anything a later op still needs.
let FloatEqualityOrderedReg = X64Register.rcx
let FloatEqualityParityReg = X64Register.rdx

// Map a Std comparison predicate to the x64 condition code its `jcc`/`setcc` tests. ONE table
// per FAMILY (not one encoder per predicate), and it drives BOTH consumers of a predicate: the
// fused `jcc` and the materializing `setcc`. They therefore cannot disagree about what `<`
// means.
//
// ⭐ THE FAMILY IS CHOSEN BY THE OPERAND TYPE, NOT BY THE PREDICATE — and it always was. It
// only looked otherwise while every Std compare was a signed i64. `cmp` on two i64s is a
// subtraction that answers in OF/SF, which the SIGNED JL/JG family reads. `ucomisd` leaves
// OF=SF=AF=0 always and answers in ZF/PF/CF — the same three flags an UNSIGNED compare writes
// — so a float compare branches with the UNSIGNED JB/JA family, and the same `StdCmpPred.less`
// lowers to `jl` for an i64 and (via the operand swap) `ja` for an f64. A float compare
// lowered to `jl` would read flags `ucomisd` never wrote.
// `isFloat` rather than the whole `StdType`, and the distinction is the ONLY thing the operand
// type decides here — `cmp` vs `ucomisd` is a flag-semantics question, not a width one. Taking
// the projection lets the FUSED-branch caller answer it from a dense bool column instead of
// carrying a full type per compare that every reader would throw all but one bit of away (see
// `fusedFloatCmps`). The contract's point stands unchanged: the family is chosen by the operand
// type and never by the predicate.
function condCodeForPred(pred StdCmpPred, isFloat bool) returns X64CondCode
	if isFloat 'float'
		return floatCondCodeForPred(pred)
	end 'float'
	return signedCondCodeForPred(pred)
end 'condCodeForPred'

// The SIGNED integer condition codes — every Std integer is a signed i64.
function signedCondCodeForPred(pred StdCmpPred) returns X64CondCode
	return match pred 'map'
		eq gives X64CondCode.equal
		ne gives X64CondCode.notEqual
		lt gives X64CondCode.less
		gt gives X64CondCode.greater
		le gives X64CondCode.lessEqual
		ge gives X64CondCode.greaterEqual
	end 'map'
end 'signedCondCodeForPred'

// The UNSIGNED condition codes — the family `ucomisd` answers in (ZF/PF/CF), and the exact
// mirror of `signedCondCodeForPred`. `cmp`'s signed family reads OF/SF, which `ucomisd` leaves
// at 0 always, so a float compare must read this one.
function unsignedCondCodeForPred(pred StdCmpPred) returns X64CondCode
	return match pred 'map'
		eq gives X64CondCode.equal
		ne gives X64CondCode.notEqual
		lt gives X64CondCode.below
		gt gives X64CondCode.above
		le gives X64CondCode.belowEqual
		ge gives X64CondCode.aboveEqual
	end 'map'
end 'unsignedCondCodeForPred'

// ⭐⭐ THE ONE TABLE THE WHOLE FLOAT COMPARE LOWERING IS DERIVED FROM: the predicate a float
// compare is ACTUALLY emitted as, once its operands have been put in the order `ucomisd` needs.
//
// A float `a < b` is emitted as `ucomisd b, a` + `ja` — i.e. it is lowered AS `b > a`. So this
// maps `lt` to `gt` and `le` to `ge`, and leaves the other four alone. Both facts the lowering
// needs fall out of it: WHETHER to swap (`floatCmpSwapsOperands` = "did this table move the
// predicate?") and WHICH condition code (`floatCondCodeForPred` = the unsigned code OF the
// swapped predicate). They cannot disagree, because there is only one of them.
//
// ⭐ WHY SWAP AT ALL — IT BUYS THE NaN ANSWER FOR NOTHING. IEEE-754 requires every relational
// predicate be FALSE when either operand is NaN, and `ucomisd` SETS CF on unordered precisely so
// the `above` family gets that free: `ja` needs CF=0 and `jae` needs CF=0, so both are NOT TAKEN
// on a NaN — correct, with no parity test. The `below` family is exactly the opposite: `jb` needs
// CF=1, which unordered SETS, so `a < b` lowered to `jb` would be TAKEN on a NaN and answer TRUE
// where IEEE demands FALSE. `jbe` is worse still — unordered sets ZF too.
//
// ⇒ AND THAT IS NOW STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN REMEMBERED: this table's RANGE never contains `lt` or
// `le`, so `floatCondCodeForPred` can never produce `below`/`belowEqual`. The NaN-wrong family is
// UNREACHABLE for floats by construction, not by a rule someone has to keep in mind.
//
// `==`/`!=` map to themselves and are not helped: they are SYMMETRIC, so swapping their operands
// changes nothing at all. They need the parity bit instead.
//
// ⚠ A `jb` lowering PASSES the obvious test — `3.5 < 9.5` is true either way. Only a NaN operand
// tells the two apart, which is what `lt-nan-is-false` exists for.
function floatCmpEmittedPred(pred StdCmpPred) returns StdCmpPred
	return match pred 'p'
		lt gives StdCmpPred.gt
		le gives StdCmpPred.ge
		eq gives StdCmpPred.eq
		ne gives StdCmpPred.ne
		gt gives StdCmpPred.gt
		ge gives StdCmpPred.ge
	end 'p'
end 'floatCmpEmittedPred'

// The FLOAT condition code: the UNSIGNED code of the predicate actually emitted. DERIVED, so it
// cannot drift from the operand order — see `floatCmpEmittedPred`.
//
// ⚠ `eq`/`ne` ARE NOT COMPLETE ON THEIR OWN. Both alias with UNORDERED — `ucomisd` reports a NaN
// as ZF=PF=CF=1, which is "equal" in every bit but PF — so the parity bit is a SECOND condition
// that no single condition code can carry. `lowerFloatEqualityBranch` supplies it as a second
// BLOCK; `materializeFloatEquality` folds it into the byte. This gives only the ORDERED half of
// those two, and is wrong if used without the other.
function floatCondCodeForPred(pred StdCmpPred) returns X64CondCode
	return unsignedCondCodeForPred(floatCmpEmittedPred(pred))
end 'floatCondCodeForPred'

// Whether a float compare emits its operands SWAPPED — true exactly when the lowering had to
// move the predicate to reach the NaN-correct family. DERIVED from the same one table, so the
// swap and the condition code are one decision and not two that must agree.
function floatCmpSwapsOperands(pred StdCmpPred) returns bool
	return floatCmpEmittedPred(pred) != pred
end 'floatCmpSwapsOperands'

// `==`/`!=` — the two SYMMETRIC predicates, and exactly the two the operand swap cannot help.
// They are the float compares that need the parity bit, and therefore a second jump.
function predicateIsEquality(pred StdCmpPred) returns bool
	return match pred 'p'
		eq gives true
		ne gives true
		lt gives false
		gt gives false
		le gives false
		ge gives false
	end 'p'
end 'predicateIsEquality'

// ⚠ THE WHOLE SSE LOWERING IS SCALAR-DOUBLE, AND THIS IS WHERE IT SAYS SO. `movsd`, `addsd`,
// `divsd`, `ucomisd` and an 8-byte `.rdata` island: every one of them is the `sd` (scalar-double)
// form. An `f32` is FOUR bytes and needs the `ss` family throughout, so it is a real GAP and not a
// spelling — and it would fail SILENTLY in the worst place: `lowerFloatConst` sizes its constant
// island from the TYPE (4 bytes for an f32) while `movsdRegRip` unconditionally loads EIGHT,
// reading half of whatever constant was laid down next.
//
// Nothing produces one — Maxon's `float` IS f64, and `x64AccessWidthFor` says the same thing for
// the memory band — so this is unreachable today. It is asserted at each float lowering entry
// anyway, because `stdTypeIsFloat` (the test that routes values here) is true of f32, so the day
// one exists this fails loudly at the door instead of quietly in the constant pool.
function requireScalarDouble(t StdType, what String)
	if t != StdType.f64 'notDouble'
		panic("{what}: {t.name} reached the SSE lowering, which is scalar-DOUBLE throughout (movsd/addsd/ucomisd + an 8-byte .rdata island) — an f32 needs the `ss` family and a 4-byte island, and would otherwise load 8 bytes out of a 4-byte constant. Maxon's `float` is f64, so nothing produces one.")
	end 'notDouble'
end 'requireScalarDouble'

// The parity condition a materialized float equality folds in — the `setcc` that isolates the
// UNORDERED answer so the other `setcc`'s aliasing can be corrected.
//
// `==` wants `setnp` (keep only the ORDERED answers, since `sete` is also true for a NaN); `!=`
// wants `setp` (ADD the unordered answer, since `setne` is false for a NaN and IEEE says `!=` is
// true there). See `materializeFloatEquality`.
function parityCondForEquality(pred StdCmpPred) returns X64CondCode
	return match pred 'p'
		eq gives X64CondCode.noParity
		ne gives X64CondCode.parity
		lt to ge panic("parityCondForEquality: {pred.name} is an ORDERED float predicate — `ucomisd` sets CF on unordered so the `above` family is already NaN-correct, and there is no parity bit to fold")
	end 'p'
end 'parityCondForEquality'

// The successor UNORDERED decides for — the target of the split's FIRST block `jp`.
//
// `nan == nan` is FALSE, so an unordered `==` takes the ELSE edge. `nan != nan` is TRUE — IEEE's
// one predicate that HOLDS on unordered — so an unordered `!=` takes the THEN edge. That is the
// whole of what the parity jump decides, and it is why the two are mirror images rather than one
// jump with a different condition code.
function parityTargetOf(pred StdCmpPred, thenBlockId BlockId, elseBlockId BlockId) returns BlockId
	return match pred 'p'
		eq gives elseBlockId
		ne gives thenBlockId
		lt to ge panic("parityTargetOf: {pred.name} is an ORDERED float predicate — it is answered by a single `ja`/`jae` on swapped operands and has no parity jump at all (see floatCmpSwapsOperands)")
	end 'p'
end 'parityTargetOf'

// Record `condId` as fusable, but only when `pred`'s compare is the one that DEFINES it.
//
// NO `Optional`-shaped return anywhere in this analysis, and that is a MEASURED constraint
// rather than a style preference. A payload-carrying union is HEAP-BOXED in Maxon (see
// StdDialect's header), and the natural spelling of the two questions this analysis asks —
// "what value does this compare define?" and "what predicate does it carry?" — asks them
// ONCE PER STD OP, which puts a fresh heap object on every op of every function the backend
// lowers. The first cut of this pass did exactly that, and `scale-test` charged it ~3,500
// allocations on the SMALLEST rung. So every classifier here either returns a `bool` or
// writes its answer through an accumulator it is handed, and allocates nothing.
// Both columns are written HERE, by this one call, which is what keeps them in lockstep — the
// predicate and the float-ness of the compare carrying it can never disagree, because nothing
// can write one without the other.
function fuseIfDefines(fused CmpPredMap, floatCmps StdValueUseSet, defId ValueId, condId ValueId, pred StdCmpPred, operandType StdType)
	if defId != condId 'someOtherCompare'
		return
	end 'someOtherCompare'
	fused.upsert(condId, value: pred)
	if stdTypeIsFloat(operandType) 'float'
		floatCmps.insert(condId)
	end 'float'
end 'fuseIfDefines'

// The three group-2 shift instructions x64 has, and the whole of what the shift lowering needs
// to know about a Std shift opcode. Named for the INSTRUCTIONS rather than for the opcodes,
// because the mapping between the two is exactly the fact worth stating once: `shl`→SHL,
// `shr`→SAR (arithmetic — a signed left operand), `shrLogical`→SHR (zero-filling — an unsigned
// one). See `shiftInstructionOf`.
enum ShiftInstruction
	shl
	sar
	shr
end 'ShiftInstruction'

// The label prefix every f64 constant island carries. It is NOT one of `classifyGlobalLabel`'s
// writable prefixes (`__data_`, `__slab_`, `__mm_`, …), so a float constant classifies as
// read-only `.rdata` — which is what it is: an immutable IEEE bit pattern the code only ever
// loads. The classification is by NAME, so the prefix is the whole of what puts it there.
let FloatConstantLabelPrefix = "__fconst_"

// The label every `ordered` block carries — the second half of a split float equality (see
// `reserveFloatEqualityBlocks`). A file-scope constant, beside `CritSplitLabel`'s reasoning: one
// slice, shared by every split, rather than a fresh allocation per branch on the lowering's path.
let FloatOrderedLabel = b"fordered"

// `globalData` is threaded in because a float CONSTANT is `.rdata` content, and `.rdata` is
// resolved at concat time against this same table — the one the `.data` globals already live in.
// Registering here (where the constant is first seen) and resolving there (where sections are laid
// out) is exactly the split `leaRegGlobal` already uses for `.data`; the float path reuses it
// rather than growing a second table with a second offset index.
export function lowerStdToX64(stdModule StdModule, globalData GlobalDataTable) returns TargetModule
	var lowering = StdToX64Lowering.create(stdModule, globalData: globalData)
	lowering.prepareSkeleton()
	// BY INDEX, never by iteration: `lowerFunction` MUTATES the function it lowers — the
	// float-equality split appends to its `blockRefs` and bumps its `nextBlockId` — so no
	// `srcFunc` borrowed out of `stdModule.functions` may be handed to it. This is the rule
	// `splitCriticalEdges` states for the identical reason, and the borrow checker enforces it
	// (E3019).
	for funcIdx in 0 upto stdModule.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		lowering.lowerFunction(funcIdx)
	end 'eachFunc'
	return lowering.targetModule
end 'lowerStdToX64'

type StdToX64Lowering
	var stdModule as StdModule
	// The backend's data table — where a float constant's `.rdata` island is registered. Shared
	// with concat, which resolves each `movsdRegRip`'s label against it once sections are laid out.
	var globalData as GlobalDataTable
	// Exported so the free-function entry can read the finished module after
	// driving the per-function loop (M5's fan-out driver reads it too).
	export var targetModule as TargetModule
	// Per-function `cmp`-value → predicate table for the fused compare/branch
	// lowering. Reset at the start of each function (ValueIds are function-local).
	var cmpPreds as CmpPredMap
	// ⭐ THE FUSED COMPARES WHOSE OPERANDS ARE FLOATS — `cmpPreds`' second column, written at the
	// same instant by the same call (`fuseIfDefines`).
	//
	// It stores the QUESTION ITS READERS ACTUALLY ASK, and that is the whole design. Both of them
	// — the condition-code family and the parity split — need to know only whether the compare is
	// a float one; nobody asks f32-vs-f64 or i8-vs-i64. A `Map with (ValueId, StdType)` here would
	// store a full type per compare so that every reader could immediately throw all but one bit
	// of it away, and MEASURED it cost ~4.7 allocations PER FUSED COMPARE — ~1,300 of a +1,528
	// rung-0 delta — on a corpus that contains no float at all. A column nobody reads is not free.
	//
	// A dense bool column (`StdValueUseSet`), so an all-integer function inserts NOTHING and the
	// backing array is never allocated: the cost is now zero for programs that use no floats,
	// rather than proportional to their compares.
	//
	// ⚠ IT IS A SUBSET OF `cmpPreds`' KEYS AND IS ONLY EVER CONSULTED AFTER A `cmpPreds` HIT. That
	// is what makes `settleFloatEqualityCondition`'s un-fuse safe without removing from it (there is no
	// `remove` on a dense set): dropping the `cmpPreds` entry makes the entry here unreachable,
	// because every reader gates on the predicate lookup first.
	var fusedFloatCmps as StdValueUseSet
	// The `ordered` block minted for each float-equality branch — see OrderedBlockIdMap and
	// `reserveFloatEqualityBlocks`. Reset per function with the two columns above.
	var orderedBlockIds as OrderedBlockIdMap
	// Every value this function reads AS A VALUE — out of a REGISTER, rather than as the EFLAGS
	// a fused branch tests. A compare whose result lands in here needs a `setcc` to write its
	// boolean into a register; one that does not stays in the flags.
	//
	// It is deliberately NOT narrowed to just the compare results. The only thing that ever
	// asks is a compare, about ITSELF, so narrowing it could not change a single answer — and
	// would cost a second full walk over every op of every function to do it.
	//
	// Computed once per function, BEFORE the op walk, because a compare is lowered long before
	// the phi edge or the later `if` that reads it is reached. INDEPENDENT of `cmpPreds`: a
	// compare can be both fused (its own branch reads its flags) AND read as a value (a `let`
	// binds it), and then it emits `cmp` + `setcc` + `jcc` — sound because `setcc` and `movzx`
	// READ the flags rather than writing them.
	var valuesReadFromRegisters as StdValueUseSet

	static function create(stdModule StdModule, globalData GlobalDataTable) returns StdToX64Lowering
		return Self{stdModule: stdModule, globalData: globalData, targetModule: TargetModule.create(), cmpPreds: CmpPredMap.create(), fusedFloatCmps: StdValueUseSet.create(), orderedBlockIds: OrderedBlockIdMap.create(), valuesReadFromRegisters: StdValueUseSet.create()}
	end 'create'

	// Whole-module setup: relocate every StdModule block 1:1 into `targetModule.blocks`
	// (preserving the flat-block indices `IrFunction.blockRefs` references) and
	// transfer every function (its StdReturnType / paramTypes carry across
	// unchanged). Must run before any lowerFunction call.
	//
	// Both `blockArgs` (the phis) AND `branchEdges` (each predecessor's per-phi
	// incoming values) are carried across: the register allocator consumes them
	// DIRECTLY and does SSA destruction after coloring (EliminatePhis is retired),
	// so the phi model must survive into the Target tier intact.
	//
	// WHAT THE TARGET TIER OWNS, AND WHY. It owns each block's `BranchEdgeArray` and
	// the `BranchEdge` objects in it, because it REPLACES them: `extractPredEdge`
	// rebuilds the array to move an edge onto a split block, and `rewriteEdgeArgsIn`
	// swaps an edge's `argIds` for a private copy before repointing it at a reload.
	// Both are field assignments, so they must land on Target-owned objects.
	// It does NOT own `blockArgs` or the `argIds` arrays — nothing here mutates those
	// in place, so they are SHARED with the Std tier (and through it the Maxon module
	// the parse memo holds). See IrBlock's header for the full ownership rules.
	function prepareSkeleton()
		for srcBlock in self.stdModule.blocks 'relocateBlocks'
			var dstBlock = IrBlock.create(srcBlock.id, label: srcBlock.label)
			dstBlock.blockArgs = srcBlock.blockArgs
			for srcEdge in srcBlock.branchEdges 'eachEdge'
				dstBlock.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(srcEdge.targetBlockId, argIds: srcEdge.argIds))
			end 'eachEdge'
			self.targetModule.blocks.push(dstBlock)
		end 'relocateBlocks'

		for srcFunc in self.stdModule.functions 'transferFuncs'
			self.targetModule.functions.push(srcFunc)
		end 'transferFuncs'
	end 'prepareSkeleton'

	// Per-function lowering: walk the function's blocks and lower each op into the
	// matching target block (indexed by the same blockRef the function carries).
	// The function's StdReturnType drives whether the return materializes an R8 move.
	// THREE STRICTLY SEQUENTIAL PHASES, and the split between them is a BORROW boundary rather
	// than a stylistic one: phase 2 MUTATES the function (it appends blocks), so neither the reads
	// before it nor the walk after it may hold a borrow of it across it. The function is
	// therefore threaded by INDEX and re-fetched inside each phase — the discipline
	// `splitCriticalEdgesInFunc` follows for the same reason, and which E3019 enforces.
	function lowerFunction(funcIdx FuncIndex)
		self.prepareFunctionTables(funcIdx)

		// ⚠ CAPTURED BEFORE PHASE 2, which appends its `ordered` refs to this very array. The walk
		// must cover exactly the blocks the STD module holds: an `ordered` block exists only in
		// `targetModule.blocks`, so its ref is out of range in `stdModule.blocks` and the walk
		// would panic on it — and it is already emitted COMPLETE, so revisiting it would lower the
		// original block's ops into it a second time.
		let originalBlockCount = self.stdBlockCountOf(funcIdx)
		self.orderedBlockIds = self.reserveFloatEqualityBlocks(funcIdx)
		self.lowerFunctionBlocks(funcIdx, originalBlockCount: originalBlockCount)
	end 'lowerFunction'

	// Phase 1 — the per-function analyses, all READS. Fresh tables each time: a `cmp`'s ValueId
	// only means anything within its own function, and both the fused branch and the value uses
	// that consume it are in the same function. All are computed BEFORE the op walk, because a
	// compare is lowered long before the branch or the phi edge that decides what it must produce.
	function prepareFunctionTables(funcIdx FuncIndex)
		let srcFunc = self.stdModule.getFunc(funcIdx)
		self.cmpPreds = CmpPredMap.create()
		self.fusedFloatCmps = StdValueUseSet.create()
		self.computeFusedCmps(srcFunc, fused: self.cmpPreds, floatCmps: self.fusedFloatCmps)
		self.valuesReadFromRegisters = self.computeValuesReadFromRegisters(srcFunc)
	end 'prepareFunctionTables'

	// The function's block count as the STD module knows it — a scoped read, so the caller holds
	// no borrow of the function across the mint that follows.
	function stdBlockCountOf(funcIdx FuncIndex) returns BlockCount
		let srcFunc = self.stdModule.getFunc(funcIdx)
		return srcFunc.blockRefs.count()
	end 'stdBlockCountOf'

	// Phase 3 — the op walk. `originalBlockCount` bounds it to the Std module's own blocks; see
	// `lowerFunction`.
	function lowerFunctionBlocks(funcIdx FuncIndex, originalBlockCount BlockCount)
		let srcFunc = self.stdModule.getFunc(funcIdx)
		for i in 0 upto originalBlockCount 'eachBlock'
			let blockRef = try srcFunc.blockRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("lowerStdToX64: blockRefs.get OOB at {i} despite the captured bound")
			let srcBlock = try self.stdModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("lowerStdToX64: blockRef from srcFunc.blockRefs not in stdModule.blocks")
			var dstBlock = try self.targetModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("lowerStdToX64: targetModule.blocks 1:1 with stdModule.blocks but get({blockRef}) failed")

			for opIdx in srcBlock.opRefs 'eachOp'
				let stdOp = try self.stdModule.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("lowerStdToX64: opIdx from srcBlock.opRefs not in stdModule.ops")
				self.lowerBodyOp(stdOp, dstBlock: dstBlock, returnType: srcFunc.returnType)
			end 'eachOp'

			if srcBlock.hasTerminatorOp() 'hasTerm'
				let termOp = try self.stdModule.ops.get(srcBlock.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("lowerStdToX64: terminatorIndex not in stdModule.ops")
				self.lowerTerminatorOp(termOp, dstBlock: dstBlock, returnType: srcFunc.returnType, blockRef: blockRef)
			end 'hasTerm'
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'lowerFunctionBlocks'

	// ==========================================================================
	// Compare/branch fusion — which compares keep their boolean in the FLAGS, and
	// which must write it to a REGISTER
	// ==========================================================================

	// The compares whose EFLAGS the branch terminating their own block will read — keyed to
	// the predicate that branch's `jcc` must test. `lowerCondBranch` fuses exactly the
	// condition values found here and nothing else.
	//
	// PURELY LOCAL, and that is precisely what makes it sound: x64 EFLAGS survive neither a
	// block boundary nor an intervening flag-clobbering instruction, so the flags a `jcc`
	// reads are ALWAYS those of the LAST flag-clobbering op before it. Fusion is legal
	// exactly when that op is the compare whose result the branch is testing — no more, no
	// less. Nothing about how many OTHER things read the compare enters into it (a compare
	// that is both fused and materialized emits `cmp` + `setcc` + `jcc`, which is correct
	// because `setcc`/`movzx` read the flags rather than writing them).
	//
	// This is the guard that used to be a `panic` ("`condBranch` on a value no `cmp`
	// defined"): a boolean condition that is a call result, a parameter or a merge phi has
	// no flags to fuse, and it is now LOWERED (`cmp reg, 0` + `jne`) rather than rejected.
	//
	// ⚠ THE PRECONDITION NOBODY HAD WRITTEN DOWN, AND THE WHOLE THING RESTS ON IT: this scan
	// runs on STD ops, but the REGISTER ALLOCATOR later inserts TARGET ops into the very
	// window it just proved safe — the gap between a fused `cmp` and its `jcc`. The fusion
	// survives only because **every op the allocator can insert there is flag-neutral**:
	//
	//     storeSlotReg · loadRegSlot (spill/reload) · movRegReg (phi copies, reuse copies) ·
	//     xchgRegReg (permutation cycles) · a REMATERIALIZED `const` (`constMaterializationOp`,
	//     a bare `mov r, imm`)
	//
	// Every one is a `mov`-class instruction that writes no EFLAGS. (`prologue`/`epilogue` DO
	// write flags — they are a `sub`/`add rsp` — but they land at a function's entry and exit
	// blocks, never inside a compare/branch window.)
	//
	// ⇒ **IF A FLAG-WRITING OP IS EVER ADDED TO WHAT THE ALLOCATOR CAN INSERT, THIS FUSION
	// BECOMES A SILENT MISCOMPILE** — a `jcc` would read flags that op had already destroyed,
	// and the Std-tier scan cannot see it coming, because the op does not exist at Std tier.
	// The likeliest candidate is a spill/reload growing an address computation, or a memzero
	// (whose size ladder `cmp`s — see ARCHITECTURE.md). Re-check this list then.
	// Fills BOTH fusion columns in one walk — see `fusedFloatCmps` for why the float-ness is a
	// column beside the predicate rather than a field inside it. They are handed in rather than
	// returned so the pair can only be created together.
	function computeFusedCmps(func IrFunction, fused CmpPredMap, floatCmps StdValueUseSet)
		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try self.stdModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("computeFusedCmps: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in stdModule.blocks")
			if not block.hasTerminatorOp() 'noTerm'
				continue
			end 'noTerm'
			let term = try self.stdModule.ops.get(block.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("computeFusedCmps: terminatorIndex not in stdModule.ops")
			match term 'term'
				condBranch(condId, _, _) then self.recordFusableCompare(block, condId: condId, fused: fused, floatCmps: floatCmps)
				const to mod then break 'term'
				branch to storeIndirect then break 'term'
			end 'term'
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'computeFusedCmps'

	// Record `condId` as fusable iff the LAST flag-clobbering body op of `block` is a
	// compare that DEFINES it. Walked backward from the end of the body, because that is
	// the order the flags are overwritten in.
	function recordFusableCompare(block IrBlock, condId ValueId, fused CmpPredMap, floatCmps StdValueUseSet)
		var i = block.opRefs.count()
		while i > 0 'scanBackward'
			i = i - 1
			let opIdx = try block.opRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("recordFusableCompare: opRefs.get OOB at {i}")
			let op = try self.stdModule.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("recordFusableCompare: opIdx {opIdx} not in stdModule.ops")
			if not op.rawValue.clobbersFlags 'flagsPreserved'
				continue
			end 'flagsPreserved'

			// The first flag-clobbering op found walking backward IS the one whose flags the
			// branch will read. If it is not the compare being tested, nothing is fusable —
			// and the scan stops HERE rather than looking further back, because whatever was
			// there had its flags overwritten by this very op. The two compare variants answer
			// identically: `foldConstOperands` moved the right operand into an immediate, which
			// changed the instruction, not what the compare means.
			match op 'isTheCompare'
				cmp(defId, _, _, pred, operandType) then fuseIfDefines(fused, floatCmps: floatCmps, defId: defId, condId: condId, pred: pred, operandType: operandType)
				// `cmpImm` is INTEGER BY CONSTRUCTION and `i64` is not a guess here: it carries no
				// `operandType` field precisely because no SSE form takes an immediate, so
				// `foldConstOperands` refuses a float rhs and a float compare can never reach this
				// variant. The absence of the field IS the statement that the type is integral.
				cmpImm(defId, _, _, pred) then fuseIfDefines(fused, floatCmps: floatCmps, defId: defId, condId: condId, pred: pred, operandType: StdType.i64)
				const to unaryOp then break 'isTheCompare'
				binOpImm then break 'isTheCompare'
				// Extended past `retVoid` to take in the memory band. It is reached only for an op
				// that already answered `clobbersFlags: true`, and no memory op does — so these arms
				// are unreachable in practice and must still be NAMED rather than swallowed: the day
				// a flag-writing memory op exists, "is it the compare?" is a question to answer here.
				div to storeIndirect then break 'isTheCompare'
			end 'isTheCompare'
			return
		end 'scanBackward'
	end 'recordFusableCompare'

	// The compare results this function reads as a VALUE — each needs a `setcc` to put its
	// boolean in a register. A value use is any operand of any op, or any phi branch-edge
	// argument, EXCEPT the condition of a branch that `computeFusedCmps` already resolved to
	// read that compare's FLAGS (which is a use of the flags, not of a register).
	//
	// The exception is per-BLOCK, not per-value, and it has to be: one compare's result can
	// feed the `condBranch` of its own block (fused, no register needed) AND the `condBranch`
	// of a LATER block (`if r … end / if r … end`), where the flags are long gone and the
	// value must be in a register. Skipping every `condBranch` use unconditionally would miss
	// that second one and leave the branch reading a value nothing ever wrote.
	function computeValuesReadFromRegisters(func IrFunction) returns StdValueUseSet
		var readAsValue = StdValueUseSet.create()
		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try self.stdModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("computeValuesReadFromRegisters: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in stdModule.blocks")

			for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachBodyOp'
				let op = try self.stdModule.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("computeValuesReadFromRegisters: opIdx {opIdx} not in stdModule.ops")
				collectStdOpUses(op, referenced: readAsValue)
			end 'eachBodyOp'

			// A phi operand is a genuine register read: SSA destruction turns it into a `mov`
			// on the edge, and a compare feeding one (the RHS of a short-circuit `and`/`or`)
			// must therefore have been materialized.
			for edge in block.branchEdges 'eachEdge'
				for argId in edge.argIds 'eachArg'
					readAsValue.insert(argId)
				end 'eachArg'
			end 'eachEdge'

			self.collectTerminatorValueUses(block, readAsValue: readAsValue)
		end 'eachBlock'

		return readAsValue
	end 'computeValuesReadFromRegisters'

	// Add the terminator's value uses to `readAsValue` — unless it is a `condBranch` whose
	// condition this block FUSES, in which case it consumes EFLAGS and not a register.
	function collectTerminatorValueUses(block IrBlock, readAsValue StdValueUseSet)
		if not block.hasTerminatorOp() 'noTerm'
			return
		end 'noTerm'
		let term = try self.stdModule.ops.get(block.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("collectTerminatorValueUses: terminatorIndex not in stdModule.ops")
		if self.terminatorReadsFusedFlags(term) 'flagsOnly'
			return
		end 'flagsOnly'
		collectStdOpUses(term, referenced: readAsValue)
	end 'collectTerminatorValueUses'

	// True iff `term` is a conditional branch on a compare this function FUSES — so it reads
	// that compare's flags, not its value. A `bool`, not an optional condition value: this is
	// asked once per block, and a payload-carrying union would heap-box the answer at each
	// (see `fuseIfDefines`).
	function terminatorReadsFusedFlags(term StdOp) returns bool
		return match term 'term'
			condBranch(condId, _, _) gives self.cmpPreds.contains(condId)
			const to mod gives false
			branch to storeIndirect gives false
		end 'term'
	end 'terminatorReadsFusedFlags'

	// Lower a regular (non-terminator) Std op. `ret` reaching here is the rare
	// mid-block form (LowerMaxonToStd emits it as a body op for a partially-
	// terminated block); it lowers the same as the terminator form, just appended.
	function lowerBodyOp(op StdOp, dstBlock IrBlock, returnType StdReturnType)
		match op 'lower'
			const(resultId, value, valueType) then self.lowerConst(resultId, value: value, valueType: valueType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			binOp(resultId, lhs, rhs, opcode, operandType) then self.lowerBinOp(resultId, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			unaryOp(resultId, operand, opcode, operandType) then self.lowerUnaryOp(resultId, operand: operand, opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			cmp(resultId, lhs, rhs, pred, operandType) then self.lowerCmp(resultId, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, pred: pred, operandType: operandType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			binOpImm(resultId, lhs, imm, opcode) then self.lowerBinOpImm(resultId, lhs: lhs, imm: imm, opcode: opcode, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			cmpImm(resultId, lhs, imm, pred) then self.lowerCmpImm(resultId, lhs: lhs, imm: imm, pred: pred, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			div(resultId, lhs, rhs) then self.lowerDivMod(resultId, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, resultReg: X64Register.rax, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			mod(resultId, lhs, rhs) then self.lowerDivMod(resultId, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, resultReg: X64Register.rdx, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			param(resultId, index, _) then self.lowerParam(resultId, index: index, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			call(resultId, callee, args) then self.lowerCall(resultId, callee: callee, args: args, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			ret(retValId) then self.lowerReturn(retValId, dstBlock: dstBlock, returnType: returnType, asTerminator: false)
			retVoid then self.lowerVoidReturn(dstBlock, returnType: returnType, asTerminator: false)
			condBranch then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `condBranch` appeared as a body op (lowering bug — a branch is always a block terminator)")
			branch then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `branch` appeared as a body op (lowering bug — a branch is always a block terminator)")
			globalAddr(resultId, label) then self.lowerGlobalAddr(resultId, label: label, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			loadIndirect(resultId, addrId, offset, valueType) then self.lowerLoadIndirect(resultId, addrId: addrId, offset: offset, valueType: valueType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			storeIndirect(addrId, offset, valueId, valueType) then self.lowerStoreIndirect(addrId, offset: offset, valueId: valueId, valueType: valueType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		end 'lower'
	end 'lowerBodyOp'

	// Lower a block terminator. M4 adds the two branch terminators; `ret` stays. A
	// `const`/`binOp`/`unaryOp`/`cmp` terminator is a lowering bug (none is a terminator).
	function lowerTerminatorOp(op StdOp, dstBlock IrBlock, returnType StdReturnType, blockRef BlockRef)
		match op 'lower'
			ret(retValId) then self.lowerReturn(retValId, dstBlock: dstBlock, returnType: returnType, asTerminator: true)
			retVoid then self.lowerVoidReturn(dstBlock, returnType: returnType, asTerminator: true)
			// `blockRef` is what `lowerCondBranch` looks its reserved `ordered` block up by — the
			// float-equality split is decided per BLOCK, before the walk, not per op.
			condBranch(condId, thenBlockId, elseBlockId) then self.lowerCondBranch(condId, thenBlockId: thenBlockId, elseBlockId: elseBlockId, dstBlock: dstBlock, blockRef: blockRef)
			branch(targetBlockId) then self.lowerBranch(targetBlockId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			const then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `const` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a constant is never a terminator)")
			binOp then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `binOp` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a binary op is never a terminator)")
			unaryOp then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `unaryOp` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a unary op is never a terminator)")
			cmp then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `cmp` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a comparison is never a terminator)")
			binOpImm then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `binOpImm` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — an arithmetic op is never a terminator)")
			cmpImm then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `cmpImm` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a comparison is never a terminator)")
			div then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `div` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a division is never a terminator)")
			mod then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `mod` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a modulo is never a terminator)")
			param then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `param` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a parameter is an entry body op)")
			call then panic("lowerStdToX64: a `call` op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a call is a body op)")
			globalAddr to storeIndirect then panic("lowerStdToX64: a memory-band op appeared as a block terminator (lowering bug — a global's address, load and store are all body ops)")
		end 'lower'
	end 'lowerTerminatorOp'

	// Materialize a constant into its virtual register. The op's own StdType picks
	// the register file (GP vs XMM) — this is what v1 encoded as a `constI64` /
	// `constF64` opcode pair and then re-encoded as MIR's `isFloat` bool.
	function lowerConst(resultId ValueId, value ParsedInt, valueType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		if stdTypeIsFloat(valueType) 'float'
			self.lowerFloatConst(resultId, value: value, valueType: valueType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			return
		end 'float'
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: constMaterializationOp(X64VReg.virtual(resultId), value: value))
	end 'lowerConst'

	// Materialize a FLOAT constant: register its IEEE bits as a `.rdata` island and load them with
	// `movsd dest, [rip + <label>]`.
	//
	// ⭐ A CONSTANT POOL, NOT AN IMMEDIATE, AND IT IS THE HARDWARE THAT SAYS SO: there is no
	// `movsd xmm, imm64`. SSE has no immediate form at ALL — the same fact that makes
	// `foldConstOperands` refuse a float rhs and `lowerBinOpImm`'s `div` arm permanently
	// unreachable. The IEEE bits ARE an integer we are holding right here, but the only routes
	// into an XMM are a load or a `movq` through a GPR, and the load costs no register.
	//
	// The bits are rendered by `scalarImage` — the very serializer the `.data` image uses for an
	// f64 global — so a constant and a global spelled `3.14` are the same eight bytes by
	// construction rather than by agreement.
	//
	// ⚠ THE OP IS NOT REMATERIALIZABLE, which is worth knowing precisely because it reads no
	// register and therefore looks like it should be. See `movsdRegRip` in TargetDialect: rebuilding
	// it needs a fresh RELOCATION, which the remat path has no way to express.
	function lowerFloatConst(resultId ValueId, value ParsedInt, valueType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		requireScalarDouble(valueType, what: "lowerFloatConst")
		let bytes = scalarImage(value, byteCount: stdTypeStorageBytes(valueType))
		let label = self.globalData.registerRdataConstant(FloatConstantLabelPrefix, bytes: bytes)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movsdRegRip(X64VReg.virtual(resultId), label: label.toByteArray()))
	end 'lowerFloatConst'

	// Lower a Std binary op (both operands registers) to x64. NO seed `mov` is emitted
	// (M5.2 deleted the two-address seed): `+` uses the 3-operand `lea dest, [lhs +
	// rhs]` (independent dest, no copy, no flags); `-`/`*` use the genuinely two-address
	// `sub`/`imul` whose operand model declares `dest` a REUSE-of-`lhs` def, so the
	// ALLOCATOR — not ISel — inserts `mov dest, lhs` only when `lhs` outlives the op. A
	// loop-carried update like `sum = sum + i` therefore emits ONE instruction. Operand
	// ORDER matters for the non-commutative `sub`: `subRegReg(dest, lhs, rhs)` computes
	// lhs − rhs (e.g. `20 - 8` → 12).
	function lowerBinOp(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, opcode StdBinOpcode, operandType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		if stdTypeIsFloat(operandType) 'float'
			self.lowerFloatBinOp(resultId, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			return
		end 'float'

		let dest = X64VReg.virtual(resultId)
		let lhsReg = X64VReg.virtual(lhs)
		let rhsReg = X64VReg.virtual(rhs)
		match opcode 'op'
			add then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.leaRegRegReg(dest, base: lhsReg, index: rhsReg))
			sub then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.subRegReg(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			mul then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.imulRegReg(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			// The word ALU ops are two-address exactly like `sub` — same reuse-def model, so
			// the allocator (not ISel) supplies the seed copy, and only when `lhs` outlives.
			bitAnd then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.andRegReg(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			bitOr then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.orRegReg(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			bitXor then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.xorRegReg(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			// One arm for all three shifts: they differ only in WHICH group-2 instruction they
			// name, and `shiftInstructionOf` is the one place that is decided.
			shl to shrLogical then self.lowerShiftCl(dest, lhsReg: lhsReg, countId: rhs, instr: shiftInstructionOf(opcode), dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// ⚠ INTEGER `binOp(div)` IS ILLEGAL, and the Std dialect's validator says so before this
			// can fire: `binOp` is the PURE arithmetic band, and integer division is not pure —
			// `idiv` faults on a zero divisor and on `i64.min / -1`, so it must not be reordered or
			// eliminated. It has its own non-pure variant, `StdOp.div`. Reaching here means a float
			// division arrived carrying an integer operand type, which would declare a trapping
			// instruction pure.
			div then panic("lowerBinOp: `binOp(div)` with an INTEGER operand type — float division rides the pure arith band because `divsd` cannot fault, while integer division traps and is `StdOp.div`. See StdBinOpcode.")
		end 'op'
	end 'lowerBinOp'

	// Lower a FLOAT binary op to its SSE instruction. All four are genuinely two-address (`addsd
	// xmm1, xmm2` — SSE has no 3-operand form without AVX), so each declares `dest` a REUSE-of-
	// `lhs` def and the ALLOCATOR supplies `movsd dest, lhs` only when `lhs` outlives the op —
	// exactly as `sub`/`imul` do in the integer path. No ISel seed copy.
	//
	// ⚠ `+` DOES NOT GET THE `lea` TREATMENT. The integer `add` lowers to the 3-operand
	// `lea dest, [lhs + rhs]`, which is copy-free and flag-free; there is no SSE analogue, so
	// float `+` is a two-address `addsd` like the rest. Operand ORDER therefore matters for the
	// non-commutative pair: `subsd`/`divsd` compute `lhs - rhs` / `lhs / rhs`.
	//
	// ⭐ `div` IS HERE AND INTEGER DIVISION IS NOT, which looks like an exception and is not: the
	// band's membership rule is its metadata profile, and `divsd` cannot fault (IEEE answers
	// inf/nan) while `idiv` traps. The two divisions differ in the one property the band is
	// defined by. See StdBinOpcode.
	function lowerFloatBinOp(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, opcode StdBinOpcode, operandType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		requireScalarDouble(operandType, what: "lowerFloatBinOp")
		let dest = X64VReg.virtual(resultId)
		let lhsReg = X64VReg.virtual(lhs)
		let rhsReg = X64VReg.virtual(rhs)
		match opcode 'op'
			add then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.addsdRegReg(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			sub then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.subsdRegReg(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			mul then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.mulsdRegReg(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			div then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.divsdRegReg(dest, lhs: lhsReg, rhs: rhsReg))
			// The word ALU and the shifts have no float form, and it is the TYPE SYSTEM that keeps
			// them out rather than this arm: `and`/`or`/`xor`/`shl`/`shr` are integer operators, and
			// a float operand is rejected long before lowering. An f64 reaching here under one of
			// them means that check is gone.
			bitAnd to shrLogical then panic("lowerFloatBinOp: {opcode.rawValue} has no float form — the bitwise and shift operators are integer-only, and a float operand is rejected at the Maxon tier")
		end 'op'
	end 'lowerFloatBinOp'

	// Lower a shift by a VARIABLE count. x64 has no register-register shift: the count must
	// be in **CL**, and in no other register. So the count is a FIXED-REGISTER operand and
	// the sequence mirrors `lowerDivMod`'s RAX/RDX handling exactly:
	//   mov rcx, count      ; a PHYSICAL destination — the count into CL's register
	//   shl/sar dest, cl    ; two-address (dest ⟸ lhs); names RCX only IMPLICITLY
	//
	// What keeps this correct is the pair of masks, and it is worth spelling out because the
	// failure mode is silent. The `mov rcx, count` is a physical DEF of RCX, so liveness
	// forbids RCX to every value live ACROSS it — which is every value the shift and anything
	// after it still needs. And the shift op's own `implicitUses` (the RCX bit) forbids RCX to
	// its EXPLICIT operands, `lhs` and `dest`. Without that second mask, `dest` — which is
	// live only from the shift onward, and so is absent from the live-across set — could be
	// coloured RCX, and the allocator's reuse copy (`mov rcx, lhs`) would then destroy the
	// shift count between the two instructions.
	//
	// Which group-2 instruction a shift opcode names. The `cl` form and the `imm8` form BOTH
	// ask this, so a `shrLogical` cannot become a `sar` in one of them and a `shr` in the
	// other — the two forms of one shift must encode the same instruction or the constant and
	// the variable count answer differently, which is precisely the class of bug the Std tier's
	// two right-shift opcodes exist to make impossible.
	//
	// A non-shift opcode PANICS rather than defaulting: it can only be a caller that widened
	// its match arm without widening this map.
	function shiftInstructionOf(opcode StdBinOpcode) returns ShiftInstruction
		return match opcode 'o'
			shl gives ShiftInstruction.shl
			shr gives ShiftInstruction.sar
			shrLogical gives ShiftInstruction.shr
			add to bitXor panic("shiftInstructionOf: {opcode.rawValue} is not a shift — only `shl`/`shr`/`shrLogical` name a group-2 instruction")
			div panic("shiftInstructionOf: `div` is float division, not a shift — only `shl`/`shr`/`shrLogical` name a group-2 instruction")
		end 'o'
	end 'shiftInstructionOf'

	function lowerShiftCl(dest X64VReg, lhsReg X64VReg, countId ValueId, instr ShiftInstruction, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(X64VReg.physical(X64Register.rcx), srcReg: X64VReg.virtual(countId)))
		match instr 'i'
			shl then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.shlRegCl(dest, lhs: lhsReg))
			sar then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.sarRegCl(dest, lhs: lhsReg))
			shr then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.shrRegCl(dest, lhs: lhsReg))
		end 'i'
	end 'lowerShiftCl'

	// The imm8 twin of `lowerShiftCl`. The cast is CHECKED, and that is the point:
	// `constFitsBinOpImmediate` promises 0..63 for a shift, and this is where that promise is
	// collected. A fold that ever broke it would fail loudly here rather than silently
	// truncating a count.
	function lowerShiftImm8(dest X64VReg, base X64VReg, imm Imm32, instr ShiftInstruction, dstBlock IrBlock)
		let count = imm as ShiftCount
		match instr 'i'
			shl then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.shlRegImm8(dest, lhs: base, imm: count))
			sar then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.sarRegImm8(dest, lhs: base, imm: count))
			shr then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.shrRegImm8(dest, lhs: base, imm: count))
		end 'i'
	end 'lowerShiftImm8'

	// Lower a Std unary op to x64. All three are genuinely two-address (`dest = <op> src`,
	// in place), so each declares `dest` a REUSE-of-`src` def and the allocator supplies the
	// copy only when `src` outlives — no ISel seed `mov`.
	//
	// The two readings of the source keyword `not` land on two DIFFERENT instructions, and
	// the Maxon tier already chose between them from the operand's type:
	//   • `bitNot`     — `not dest` (one's complement of all 64 bits): `not 5` == -6.
	//   • `logicalNot` — `xor dest, 1`: a bool is 0/1, so flipping bit 0 negates it. Using
	//     the integer `not` here would make `not true` == -2, which still reads as TRUE in
	//     every condition — a silent miscompile, and the reason these are two opcodes.
	// ⚠ A FLOAT NEGATE IS NOT `neg`, AND IT DOES NOT ARRIVE HERE. `neg` is an integer two's
	// complement; negating an f64 means flipping its SIGN BIT (`xorpd` against a sign mask), which
	// is a different instruction and needs a `.rdata` mask constant. Nothing produces one yet:
	// `-x` on a float would, and the Maxon tier does not fold it to a `unaryOp(neg)` over an f64
	// — the spec corpus reaches negative floats through `0.0 - x` instead. This panics rather than
	// emitting an integer `neg` on a float register, which the class check would catch anyway, but
	// later and further from the cause.
	function lowerUnaryOp(resultId ValueId, operand ValueId, opcode StdUnaryOpcode, operandType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		if stdTypeIsFloat(operandType) 'float'
			panic("lowerUnaryOp: no float form of {opcode.rawValue} — an f64 negate flips the sign bit (`xorpd` against a .rdata mask), not the integer `neg`, and nothing lowers to one yet (P1.0d.4 reaches negative floats through `0.0 - x`)")
		end 'float'

		let dest = X64VReg.virtual(resultId)
		let src = X64VReg.virtual(operand)
		match opcode 'op'
			neg then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.negReg(dest, src: src))
			bitNot then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.notReg(dest, src: src))
			logicalNot then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.xorRegImm32(dest, lhs: src, imm: LogicalNotMask))
		end 'op'
	end 'lowerUnaryOp'

	// Lower a Std `binOpImm` (constant right operand) to a copy-free immediate form:
	// `add`/`sub` to `lea dest, [lhs + disp]` (disp = imm for add, −imm for sub — the
	// literal needs no register and the op sets no flags); `mul` to the 3-operand `imul
	// dest, lhs, imm`. None is two-address, so no reuse copy is ever needed.
	function lowerBinOpImm(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, imm Imm32, opcode StdBinOpcode, dstBlock IrBlock)
		let dest = X64VReg.virtual(resultId)
		let base = X64VReg.virtual(lhs)
		match opcode 'op'
			add then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.leaRegRegImm32(dest, base: base, disp: imm))
			// `a - imm` is `lea dest, [base - imm]`, a NEGATIVE displacement. `foldConstOperands`
			// only emits a `sub` immediate for |imm| <= i32.max, so `-imm` always fits Imm32; the
			// checked cast documents that the range-widened `0 - imm` cannot overflow here.
			sub then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.leaRegRegImm32(dest, base: base, disp: (0 - imm) as Imm32))
			mul then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.imulRegRegImm32(dest, src: base, imm: imm))
			// The word ALU immediate forms are two-address (`and dest, imm` modifies dest in
			// place), UNLIKE the `lea`/`imul` forms above whose destination is independent of
			// their source. So they carry a reuse-def, and the allocator supplies the copy.
			bitAnd then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.andRegImm32(dest, lhs: base, imm: imm))
			bitOr then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.orRegImm32(dest, lhs: base, imm: imm))
			bitXor then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.xorRegImm32(dest, lhs: base, imm: imm))
			// A CONSTANT shift count needs no RCX at all — the whole reason `foldConstOperands`
			// bothers to fold it.
			shl to shrLogical then self.lowerShiftImm8(dest, base: base, imm: imm, instr: shiftInstructionOf(opcode), dstBlock: dstBlock)
			// PERMANENTLY unreachable, and it is the hardware that says so: no SSE instruction
			// takes an immediate operand, so a float `binOp` has no immediate form to fold to
			// and `foldConstOperands` refuses one. If this ever fires, that guard is gone and
			// IEEE bit patterns are riding the integer ALU.
			div then panic("lowerBinOpImm: `div` is float division and no SSE form takes an immediate — foldConstOperands must never produce this")
		end 'op'
	end 'lowerBinOpImm'

	// --- P1.0d.5b: the data-section band ---
	//
	// All three lower 1:1 — the Std memory band and the x64 data-section band are the same three
	// ops, because x64 has exactly these three instructions. The only real work is the WIDTH
	// decision (`x64AccessWidthFor`), which is the tier boundary doing its job.

	// `globalAddr` → `lea dest, [rip + <label>]`. The address is computed, not loaded; the PE
	// writer supplies the displacement once `.data` has an RVA.
	function lowerGlobalAddr(resultId ValueId, label ByteArray, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.leaRegGlobal(X64VReg.virtual(resultId), label: label))
	end 'lowerGlobalAddr'

	// `loadIndirect` → `mov dest, [base + disp]` / `movzx dest, byte [base + disp]`.
	function lowerLoadIndirect(resultId ValueId, addrId ValueId, offset ByteOffset, valueType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.loadRegBaseDisp(X64VReg.virtual(resultId), base: X64VReg.virtual(addrId), disp: memoryDisplacement(offset), width: x64AccessWidthFor(valueType)))
	end 'lowerLoadIndirect'

	// `storeIndirect` → `mov [base + disp], src`.
	function lowerStoreIndirect(addrId ValueId, offset ByteOffset, valueId ValueId, valueType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(X64VReg.virtual(addrId), disp: memoryDisplacement(offset), src: X64VReg.virtual(valueId), width: x64AccessWidthFor(valueType)))
	end 'lowerStoreIndirect'

	// Lower a Std `div`/`mod` to the x64 `idiv` sequence. x64 `idiv r/m64` divides the
	// 128-bit RDX:RAX by the divisor, leaving the QUOTIENT in RAX and the REMAINDER in
	// RDX — RAX and RDX are FIXED registers the instruction pins. So the sequence is:
	//   mov rax, dividend   ; dividend (lhs) into RAX's low half
	//   cqo                 ; sign-extend RAX into RDX:RAX (fills the high half)
	//   idiv divisor        ; RDX:RAX / divisor -> quotient in RAX, remainder in RDX
	//   mov result, resultReg ; copy RAX (div) or RDX (mod) out to the result vreg
	// The divisor stays a VIRTUAL register — the allocator keeps it out of RAX/RDX
	// (idivReg's implicit RAX/RDX masks forbid it, see TargetLiveness), so it survives
	// both the `mov rax, dividend` and the `cqo` to be read by the `idiv`. `resultReg`
	// selects which fixed register the answer is read from: RAX for `/` (quotient), RDX
	// for `mod` (remainder) — the ONLY difference between the two, so they share this one
	// lowering rather than duplicating the four-op emit.
	//
	// idiv TRAPS (#DE) on divide-by-zero and on INT_MIN / -1 overflow. That trap is
	// delivered by the runtime fault handler (a Workstream-R / M6 deliverable), NOT by a
	// compiler guard — so `x / 0` here is correct-but-raw-#DE for now, by design.
	function lowerDivMod(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, resultReg X64Register, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(X64VReg.physical(X64Register.rax), srcReg: X64VReg.virtual(lhs)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.cqo)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.idivReg(X64VReg.virtual(rhs)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(X64VReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: X64VReg.physical(resultReg)))
	end 'lowerDivMod'

	// Lower a Std `param` to the entry move that captures the parameter's incoming ABI
	// register into its virtual register: `mov virtual(resultId), physical(argReg[index])`.
	// Modelled as a copy from a PHYSICAL source, so the existing `collectOpHints` fixed-
	// register hint (mov-to-virtual from a physical source) biases the parameter value
	// toward its own argument register — when free, the coloring lands it there and this
	// move self-elides. `collectEntryParamCaptures` finds these moves again, and the
	// cross-param forbidding it drives is what stops one capture's DESTINATION from
	// clobbering a later capture's incoming register before it is read.
	function lowerParam(resultId ValueId, index ParamCount, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(X64VReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: X64VReg.physical(argRegisterAt(index))))
	end 'lowerParam'

	// Lower a Std `call` to the x64 call sequence: move each argument into its ABI
	// register, `callDirect` the callee, then move the R8 return value into the result.
	//
	// The argument moves are emitted in slot order as plain `mov argReg[k], arg_k`
	// (physical destination, virtual source) — the same "pre-move into a fixed physical
	// register" pattern the `idiv` dividend uses, extended to N arguments. This is SOUND
	// without an explicit parallel-copy sequencer BECAUSE each `mov argReg[k], …` has a
	// physical def of `argReg[k]`, which the liveness pass adds to `forbiddenPhys` for
	// every value LIVE ACROSS that move — so an argument value live into a later move can
	// never be colored to an EARLIER argument register (the read-after-clobber class
	// `project_call_arg_parallel_copy_fix` names). Forward-order emission then reads each
	// argument's register before any later move overwrites it. The `callDirect` carries
	// no explicit operands, so its caller-saved `implicitDefs` clobber is applied ONLY to
	// values live across the call (never to the just-established argument registers), and
	// `forbidOperandsFromImplicit` is a no-op on it.
	function lowerCall(resultId ValueId, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, dstBlock IrBlock)
		for k in 0 upto args.count() 'eachArg'
			let argId = try args.get(k) otherwise panic("lowerCall: args.get OOB at {k}")
			self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(X64VReg.physical(argRegisterAt(k)), srcReg: X64VReg.virtual(argId)))
		end 'eachArg'
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.callDirect(callee))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(X64VReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: X64VReg.physical(X64Register.r8)))
	end 'lowerCall'

	// Lower a Std `cmpImm` (constant right operand) to `cmp reg, imm32` — the same two-part
	// protocol `lowerCmp` uses, so the fold that moved the right operand into an immediate
	// changes only the compare INSTRUCTION, never what the compare MEANS. The literal
	// occupies no register.
	function lowerCmpImm(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, imm Imm32, pred StdCmpPred, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.cmpRegImm32(X64VReg.virtual(lhs), imm: imm))
		// INTEGER by construction, and `i64` is not a guess: `cmpImm` carries no `operandType`
		// because no SSE form takes an immediate, so `foldConstOperands` refuses a float rhs and a
		// float compare can never reach this variant. See `recordFusableCompare`'s twin of this.
		self.materializeCompareIfRead(resultId, pred: pred, operandType: StdType.i64, dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'lowerCmpImm'

	// Lower a Std `cmp` to an x64 `cmp reg, reg`. A comparison's answer lives in the EFLAGS
	// the instruction sets, and where it goes from there depends on who reads it — the two
	// consumers are INDEPENDENT and either, both, or neither may apply:
	//
	//   • the `condBranch` terminating this block, if `computeFusedCmps` proved this compare
	//     is the last flag-clobbering op before it ⇒ `lowerCondBranch` emits a `jcc` and no
	//     boolean is ever written to a register (the common `if a > b` case);
	//   • anything that reads the result as a VALUE — a `let`, a call argument, a phi operand
	//     on a short-circuit edge, an `if` in a LATER block ⇒ `setcc` writes the boolean into
	//     its register here.
	//
	// Both at once is legal and correct: `setcc`/`movzx` READ the flags, they do not write
	// them, so the `jcc` at the terminator still sees the flags this `cmp` set.
	function lowerCmp(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, pred StdCmpPred, operandType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		if stdTypeIsFloat(operandType) 'float'
			self.lowerFloatCmp(resultId, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, pred: pred, operandType: operandType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			return
		end 'float'
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.cmpRegReg(X64VReg.virtual(lhs), rhsReg: X64VReg.virtual(rhs)))
		self.materializeCompareIfRead(resultId, pred: pred, operandType: operandType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'lowerCmp'

	// Lower a FLOAT `cmp` to `ucomisd`, with the same two independent consumers `lowerCmp`
	// describes — the fused branch reads its flags, anything reading the result as a VALUE gets a
	// `setcc` sequence.
	//
	// ⭐ THE OPERANDS MAY BE SWAPPED HERE, and that is the whole NaN story for four of the six
	// predicates. `a < b` emits `ucomisd b, a`, and `floatCondCodeForPred` answers `above` to
	// match — one decision, spelled in two places that MUST be read together (see
	// `floatCmpSwapsOperands` for why `jb` would be wrong and why the swap is free).
	function lowerFloatCmp(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, pred StdCmpPred, operandType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		requireScalarDouble(operandType, what: "lowerFloatCmp")
		let swapped = floatCmpSwapsOperands(pred)
		let cmpLhs = rhs if swapped else lhs
		let cmpRhs = lhs if swapped else rhs
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.ucomisdRegReg(X64VReg.virtual(cmpLhs), rhs: X64VReg.virtual(cmpRhs)))
		self.materializeCompareIfRead(resultId, pred: pred, operandType: operandType, dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'lowerFloatCmp'

	// Write a compare's boolean into its register — but only when something actually reads it
	// as a value (`computeValuesReadFromRegisters`). A compare read ONLY by the branch it fuses with
	// needs no register at all, which is why `if a > b` still lowers to a bare `cmp` + `jcc`.
	function materializeCompareIfRead(resultId ValueId, pred StdCmpPred, operandType StdType, dstBlock IrBlock)
		if not self.valuesReadFromRegisters.contains(resultId) 'flagsOnly'
			return
		end 'flagsOnly'
		if stdTypeIsFloat(operandType) and predicateIsEquality(pred) 'floatEquality'
			self.materializeFloatEquality(resultId, pred: pred, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			return
		end 'floatEquality'
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.setccReg(X64VReg.virtual(resultId), cond: condCodeForPred(pred, isFloat: stdTypeIsFloat(operandType))))
	end 'materializeCompareIfRead'

	// MATERIALIZE a float `==`/`!=` — the one compare whose boolean a single `setcc` cannot write.
	//
	// `ucomisd` aliases UNORDERED with EQUAL in every bit but PF, so the answer is TWO conditions
	// and the byte has to be folded from two `setcc`s:
	//
	//     ==   sete  dest ; setnp scratch ; and dest, scratch      equal AND ordered
	//     !=   setne dest ; setp  scratch ; or  dest, scratch      unequal OR unordered
	//
	// This is the SECOND lowering of the unordered edge, not a free consequence of the branch
	// one: a compare whose result is a VALUE has no jump to hang a parity test on, so the bit
	// folds into the byte instead. The ORDERED predicates need none of it — they materialize with
	// a single `seta`/`setae` on swapped operands, exactly like an integer's.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ THE FOLD RUNS ENTIRELY IN PHYSICAL REGISTERS, AND THAT IS A HARD SSA REQUIREMENT, not a
	// register-pressure preference. The obvious spelling — `setcc dest` then `and dest, scratch` —
	// DEFINES `dest` TWICE, because the two-address `and` is a REUSE-of-`lhs` def and `lhs` would
	// BE `dest`. Std ValueIds are SSA and the colorer relies on single-def (the rule `xchgRegReg`
	// is exempt from only because it is spliced in AFTER coloring); a second def would be
	// discovered as a wrong answer, not a compile error. No other lowering in this file writes a
	// vreg twice, and this one must not either.
	//
	// So the two `setcc`s and the fold land in FIXED registers, and exactly ONE `mov` publishes
	// the result into its virtual — which is `lowerDivMod`'s shape, for `lowerDivMod`'s reason:
	// the intermediate values are not SSA values at all, so they cannot violate SSA. The physical
	// defs also carry their own safety: liveness forbids both scratches to every value live ACROSS
	// them (the mechanism `lowerShiftCl` documents for RCX), so nothing the fold clobbers can be
	// something a later op still needs.
	//
	// Both `setcc`s READ the flags and write none, so the `ucomisd`'s answer survives to the
	// second one. The `and`/`or` DOES write flags — which is exactly why a float equality that is
	// also branched on is un-fused first (see `settleFloatEqualityCondition`).
	function materializeFloatEquality(resultId ValueId, pred StdCmpPred, dstBlock IrBlock)
		let ordered = X64VReg.physical(FloatEqualityOrderedReg)
		let parity = X64VReg.physical(FloatEqualityParityReg)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.setccReg(ordered, cond: floatCondCodeForPred(pred)))
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.setccReg(parity, cond: parityCondForEquality(pred)))
		self.appendParityCombine(ordered, scratch: parity, pred: pred, dstBlock: dstBlock)
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(X64VReg.virtual(resultId), srcReg: ordered))
	end 'materializeFloatEquality'

	// `==` is `equal AND ordered`; `!=` is `unequal OR unordered`. Both operands are in the 0/1
	// domain `setcc` produces, so the WORD `and`/`or` is also the logical one — the distinction
	// `LogicalNotMask` documents does not bite here.
	function appendParityCombine(dest X64VReg, scratch X64VReg, pred StdCmpPred, dstBlock IrBlock)
		match pred 'p'
			eq then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.andRegReg(dest, lhs: dest, rhs: scratch))
			ne then self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.orRegReg(dest, lhs: dest, rhs: scratch))
			lt to ge then panic("appendParityCombine: {pred.name} is an ORDERED float predicate — it materializes with a single `seta`/`setae` on swapped operands and has no parity bit to fold")
		end 'p'
	end 'appendParityCombine'

	// Lower a Std `condBranch` to `jcc <then>` + `jmp <else>`. The `jcc` is a body op and the
	// `jmp` is the block's terminator (a two-instruction two-way branch cannot fit one
	// terminator slot). An unconditional `jmp` to the else target is emitted for EVERY layout
	// so correctness never depends on block ordering (a later pass may elide a fall-through).
	//
	// THREE shapes, and which one applies is settled BEFORE the walk — by `computeFusedCmps` and
	// `settleFloatEqualityBranch` — never guessed here:
	//
	//   • A SPLIT FLOAT EQUALITY — a fused float `==`/`!=`, which reserved an `ordered` block
	//     because the parity bit is a second condition and a block may hold one. It takes
	//     precedence over both below; see the float-equality split section.
	//   • FUSED — the compare defining `condId` is the last flag-clobbering op of this block,
	//     so the flags it set are exactly the ones the `jcc` reads. The `jcc` tests that
	//     compare's predicate directly and nothing was materialized.
	//   • NOT FUSED — the condition is an ordinary boolean VALUE in a register: a `bool`
	//     parameter, a call's `bool` return, a short-circuit merge phi, a `let`-bound compare
	//     read a few statements later. It is TRUTH-TESTED against zero (`cmp reg, 0` + `jne`),
	//     which is the same instruction pair the old parser-synthesized `value != false`
	//     compare used to fold to — so `if flag` emits byte-identical code, with two fewer IR
	//     ops to get there.
	//
	// `appendCondBranch`, NOT `appendOp`: this is the ONE site in the compiler that makes a
	// block two-way, so it is the one site that records where the `jcc` landed. Without that
	// index a block's then-edge is only findable by scanning every op of the block, which is
	// what made building the CFG skeleton O(total ops) (see `CondBranch`). It also seals the
	// block: `appendOp` refuses to append past a conditional branch.
	function lowerCondBranch(condId ValueId, thenBlockId BlockId, elseBlockId BlockId, dstBlock IrBlock, blockRef BlockRef)
		let orderedId = try self.orderedBlockIds.get(blockRef) otherwise 'notFloatEquality'
			let cond = self.branchConditionCode(condId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
			self.targetModule.appendCondBranch(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.jcc(cond, targetBlockId: thenBlockId))
			self.targetModule.setTerminator(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.jmp(elseBlockId))
			return
		end 'notFloatEquality'

		self.lowerFloatEqualityBranch(condId, thenBlockId: thenBlockId, elseBlockId: elseBlockId, orderedId: orderedId, dstBlock: dstBlock)
	end 'lowerCondBranch'

	// The FIRST block of a split float equality: it asks "unordered?" and nothing else. The
	// `ucomisd` is already in this block — `lowerCmp` emitted it as a body op — so all that is
	// left is the parity jump and the fall-through into `ordered` (which
	// `reserveFloatEqualityBlocks` has already emitted, complete).
	function lowerFloatEqualityBranch(condId ValueId, thenBlockId BlockId, elseBlockId BlockId, orderedId BlockId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		let pred = try self.cmpPreds.get(condId) otherwise panic("lowerFloatEqualityBranch: v{condId} is not a fused compare, but an `ordered` block was reserved for its branch")
		let parityTarget = parityTargetOf(pred, thenBlockId: thenBlockId, elseBlockId: elseBlockId)
		self.targetModule.appendCondBranch(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.jcc(X64CondCode.parity, targetBlockId: parityTarget))
		self.targetModule.setTerminator(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.jmp(orderedId))

		// ⚠ THE OTHER PHI EDGE MUST GO, and this is the half that makes the split honest. This
		// block now branches to exactly two places: `parityTarget` and `ordered`. Its edge to the
		// OTHER original successor is stale — that successor is reached only THROUGH `ordered`
		// now, which was handed its own copy of the edge. Leaving it would have SSA destruction
		// place that phi's moves at the end of this block, on an edge it no longer takes, writing
		// a phi's register on the unordered path with the wrong incoming value. `ordered` itself
		// carries no phis (it is not a join), so the edge to it needs no `BranchEdge` at all.
		self.keepOnlyBranchEdgeIn(dstBlock, keepTarget: parityTarget)
	end 'lowerFloatEqualityBranch'

	// Drop every branch edge of `block` except the one to `keepTarget` — see the caller.
	function keepOnlyBranchEdgeIn(block IrBlock, keepTarget BlockId)
		var kept = BranchEdgeArray.create()
		for edge in block.branchEdges 'each'
			if edge.targetBlockId == keepTarget 'keep'
				kept.push(edge)
			end 'keep'
		end 'each'
		block.branchEdges = kept
	end 'keepOnlyBranchEdgeIn'

	// The condition code the branch's `jcc` tests, emitting the truth-test `cmp reg, 0` first
	// when the condition is a value rather than a fused compare's flags.
	function branchConditionCode(condId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock) returns X64CondCode
		let pred = try self.cmpPreds.get(condId) otherwise 'notFused'
			self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.cmpRegImm32(X64VReg.virtual(condId), imm: BoolFalseImm))
			return X64CondCode.notEqual
		end 'notFused'
		return condCodeForPred(pred, isFloat: self.fusedFloatCmps.contains(condId))
	end 'branchConditionCode'

	// ==========================================================================
	// The float-equality split
	// ==========================================================================
	//
	// ⭐⭐ AN f64 `==` NEEDS TWO CONDITIONAL JUMPS, AND A BLOCK MAY HAVE ONE.
	//
	// `ucomisd` reports UNORDERED (either operand NaN) as ZF=PF=CF=1 — the flags of "equal" but
	// for PF. IEEE-754 says `nan == nan` is FALSE and `nan != nan` is TRUE, so PF is the ONLY bit
	// separating those answers from equality, and a bare `je` would take the then-branch on a
	// NaN. The parity bit is a second condition, and a second condition is a second jump.
	//
	// v1 SHIPPED EXACTLY THIS AS A SILENT WRONG ANSWER, and how is worth knowing. It emitted ONE
	// block ending in TWO conditional jumps:
	//
	//     ucomisd a, b ; jp else ; jne else ; jmp then
	//
	// The IR said that block had one conditional branch and therefore ONE else edge. The machine
	// code had two. SSA destruction places a phi's copies on an edge by rewriting the jump that
	// takes it — so it found and rewired one of those jumps, and the other kept its original
	// target and bypassed the copies entirely. A phi whose value arrived on the `jp` path was
	// never written. (`project_x64_f64_compare_phi_copy_fix` — a positive double formatted as
	// negative, plus a runaway digit loop that spewed megabytes.)
	//
	// ⇒ shv2 SPLITS THE COMPARE INTO TWO BLOCKS, each ending in exactly one conditional branch:
	//
	//     cmp:     ucomisd a, b ; jp -> <the answer unordered decides> ; jmp -> ordered
	//     ordered:               je/jne -> then                        ; jmp -> else
	//
	// The machine code is the same three jumps. The difference is that the IR now SAYS what the
	// machine code does: two edges reach the far block, from two different blocks, and they ARE
	// two edges in the CFG. SSA destruction needs no float-specific case — it places copies on
	// both, because both are edges — and `IrBlock.CondBranch`'s one-conditional-branch-per-block
	// invariant is never bent. THE BUG IS NOT FIXED HERE. IT IS MADE UNREPRESENTABLE.
	//
	// specs-shv2/float-compare-branch.md puts a phi on the edge each of those jumps takes:
	// `eq-nan-else-edge-phi` rides the first block's `jp`, `ne-nan-then-edge-phi` rides it to the
	// other successor, and `eq-ordered-*-edge-phi` ride the second block's two.

	// Mint the `ordered` block of every float-equality branch in the function, emitted COMPLETE:
	// its `je`/`jne`, its `jmp`, and a copy of both of the original block's phi edges. The walk
	// is left only the original block's half, and needs nothing from here but a block id.
	//
	// ⚠ IT IS A SEPARATE PASS FOR A BORROW REASON, not for taste. Minting PUSHES to
	// `targetModule.blocks`, and the walk holds a live borrow out of that same array for the
	// whole of every block (its `dstBlock`) — the rule `moveBranchEdgeToSplit` scopes its helpers
	// apart to obey. It also pushes to `srcFunc.blockRefs`, which the walk reads by index.
	// Running it before either borrow exists is what makes both safe.
	function reserveFloatEqualityBlocks(funcIdx FuncIndex) returns OrderedBlockIdMap
		// COLLECT FIRST, THEN MINT — `splitCriticalEdgesInFunc`'s exact discipline, and for its
		// exact reason: the mint below fetches and MUTATES the function, so no borrow of it may
		// still be live, and it also appends to the very `blockRefs` this would otherwise iterate.
		// The classification is unaffected by the splits (each only adds a block), so the snapshot
		// stays valid across all of them.
		let toSplit = self.blocksNeedingFloatEqualitySplit(funcIdx)

		var reserved = OrderedBlockIdMap.create()
		for blockRef in toSplit 'eachSplit'
			reserved.upsert(blockRef, value: self.buildOrderedBlock(funcIdx, blockRef: blockRef))
		end 'eachSplit'
		return reserved
	end 'reserveFloatEqualityBlocks'

	// SETTLE EVERY FLOAT-EQUALITY BRANCH'S SHAPE, in one walk of the function's blocks: un-fuse the
	// ones whose boolean is also materialized, then collect the ones that still need a split.
	//
	// The two steps share this walk because they are one concern asked of one thing — "what shape
	// does this branch take?" — and because their ORDER is a requirement rather than a
	// coincidence: un-fusing must happen BEFORE the split test, or a branch that is about to lose
	// its fusion would still be handed an `ordered` block. Doing them per block rather than in two
	// passes makes that ordering local and structural instead of a comment about pass order, and
	// it is sound because each branch's decision reads only its OWN condition.
	//
	// It is also a scoped READ of the function (the un-fuse touches only `cmpPreds`), complete
	// before `reserveFloatEqualityBlocks` mints anything.
	function blocksNeedingFloatEqualitySplit(funcIdx FuncIndex) returns BlockRefArray
		let srcFunc = self.stdModule.getFunc(funcIdx)
		var toSplit = BlockRefArray.create()
		for blockRef in srcFunc.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			if self.settleFloatEqualityBranch(blockRef) 'needsSplit'
				toSplit.push(blockRef)
			end 'needsSplit'
		end 'eachBlock'
		return toSplit
	end 'blocksNeedingFloatEqualitySplit'

	// Settle ONE block's float-equality branch and answer whether it still needs a split. Both the
	// un-fuse and the decision live here, in one terminator read, because they are one question
	// asked of one branch — and because their order is a REQUIREMENT (un-fuse first, or a branch
	// about to lose its fusion is still handed an `ordered` block), which is structural here
	// instead of a note about pass ordering.
	function settleFloatEqualityBranch(blockRef BlockRef) returns bool
		let block = try self.stdModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("settleFloatEqualityBranch: blockRef {blockRef} not in stdModule.blocks")
		if not block.hasTerminatorOp() 'noTerm'
			return false
		end 'noTerm'
		let term = try self.stdModule.ops.get(block.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("settleFloatEqualityBranch: terminatorIndex not in stdModule.ops")
		return match term 'term'
			condBranch(condId, _, _) gives self.settleFloatEqualityCondition(condId)
			const to mod gives false
			branch to storeIndirect gives false
		end 'term'
	end 'settleFloatEqualityBranch'

	// The decision itself, for one branch condition. Three answers, and only the first needs a
	// second block:
	//
	//   • a FUSED FLOAT `==`/`!=` read ONLY by this branch ⇒ SPLIT. Its flags carry the answer and
	//     the parity bit is a second condition, so it needs a second block to test it in.
	//   • a fused float equality whose boolean is ALSO read as a value ⇒ UN-FUSE, no split. Its
	//     materialization folds the parity bit with an `and`/`or` (`materializeFloatEquality`), and
	//     that WRITES EFLAGS — destroying the very PF the split's `jp` would read. The Std-tier
	//     fusion scan cannot see this coming: it proves the `cmp` is the last flag-clobbering STD
	//     op before the branch, and the `and` does not exist at that tier. It is the hazard
	//     `computeFusedCmps`' header raises for the ALLOCATOR's inserted ops, arriving from the
	//     lowering instead. Dropping the fusion is the whole fix and costs nothing that matters:
	//     the branch then truth-tests the materialized bool (`cmp reg, 0` + `jne`), exactly as
	//     every other non-fused boolean condition already does. It is SELF-CONSISTENT — the value
	//     is in `valuesReadFromRegisters` precisely because something reads it, so the `setcc` the
	//     branch now depends on is emitted regardless of this.
	//   • anything else ⇒ no split. An UNFUSED condition is an ordinary bool in a register; an
	//     INTEGER compare has no unordered answer; an ORDERED float predicate is answered by a
	//     single `ja`/`jae` on swapped operands and needs no second jump at all.
	function settleFloatEqualityCondition(condId ValueId) returns bool
		if not self.isFusedFloatEquality(condId) 'notFloatEquality'
			return false
		end 'notFloatEquality'
		if not self.valuesReadFromRegisters.contains(condId) 'flagsOnly'
			return true
		end 'flagsOnly'

		// The entry is provably present — `isFusedFloatEquality` just read it — so the "was it
		// there?" answer carries no information and is discarded. Only `cmpPreds` is unwound, and
		// that is correct rather than a half-erasure: dropping the predicate makes this value's
		// `fusedFloatCmps` bit UNREACHABLE, because every reader of that set gates on a `cmpPreds`
		// hit first (see the field). A dense bool column has no `remove` to call anyway.
		_ = self.cmpPreds.remove(condId)
		return false
	end 'settleFloatEqualityCondition'

	// True iff `condId` is defined by a FUSED compare that is a FLOAT `==` or `!=` — the one
	// compare shape whose branch cannot be a single jump.
	function isFusedFloatEquality(condId ValueId) returns bool
		let pred = try self.cmpPreds.get(condId) otherwise 'notFused'
			return false
		end 'notFused'
		return self.fusedFloatCmps.contains(condId) and predicateIsEquality(pred)
	end 'isFusedFloatEquality'

	// Create the `ordered` block for `blockRef`'s float-equality branch, emit it complete, and
	// return its id.
	//
	// It is reached ONLY when `ucomisd` said ORDERED — the original block's `jp` has already
	// taken the unordered answer away — so its `je`/`jne` is the plain equality test with no NaN
	// case left in it. That is the whole trick: each block asks exactly ONE question.
	//
	// Every step is its own scoped helper, so no two live borrows of `targetModule.blocks` ever
	// coexist. Reads come first, then the mint, then the writes.
	function buildOrderedBlock(funcIdx FuncIndex, blockRef BlockRef) returns BlockId
		let (condId, thenBlockId, elseBlockId) = self.condBranchFieldsOf(blockRef)
		let pred = try self.cmpPreds.get(condId) otherwise panic("buildOrderedBlock: v{condId} is not a fused compare, but blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit said its block needs a split")

		// The original's phi edges, cloned BEFORE the new block exists — the walk prunes the
		// original down to its one surviving edge later, and `ordered` needs both.
		let inheritedEdges = self.cloneBranchEdgesOf(blockRef)

		let newRef = self.addOrderedBlock(funcIdx)
		let newId = self.blockIdOf(newRef)
		self.emitOrderedBlockBody(newRef, cond: floatCondCodeForPred(pred), thenBlockId: thenBlockId, elseBlockId: elseBlockId, edges: inheritedEdges)
		return newId
	end 'buildOrderedBlock'

	// Mint an empty `ordered` block: a fresh function-local id, an entry in `targetModule.blocks`,
	// and its ref registered on the function. Mirrors `addSplitBlock` (SsaDestruction), which is
	// the established way a Target-tier pass grows the CFG — including its deliberate omission of
	// `blockSourceInfo`, which the Target module does not carry (`prepareSkeleton` never fills it).
	// The function is fetched HERE, by index, and mutated directly — its borrow never escapes to a
	// caller that could still be holding one. Same shape as `addSplitBlock`.
	function addOrderedBlock(funcIdx FuncIndex) returns BlockRef
		var srcFunc = self.stdModule.getFunc(funcIdx)
		let newId = srcFunc.nextBlockId
		srcFunc.nextBlockId = srcFunc.nextBlockId + 1
		self.targetModule.blocks.push(IrBlock.create(newId, label: FloatOrderedLabel))
		let newRef = self.targetModule.blocks.count() - 1
		srcFunc.blockRefs.push(newRef)
		return newRef
	end 'addOrderedBlock'

	// Emit the `ordered` block's whole body: the ordered equality test to `then`, the `jmp` to
	// `else`, and the phi edges it inherited. Uniform for both `==` and `!=` — only `cond`
	// differs (`je` vs `jne`), because the unordered answer was already decided by the `jp`.
	function emitOrderedBlockBody(blockRef BlockRef, cond X64CondCode, thenBlockId BlockId, elseBlockId BlockId, edges BranchEdgeArray)
		var block = try self.targetModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("emitOrderedBlockBody: blockRef {blockRef} not in targetModule.blocks")
		block.branchEdges = edges
		self.targetModule.appendCondBranch(block, op: TargetOp.jcc(cond, targetBlockId: thenBlockId))
		self.targetModule.setTerminator(block, op: TargetOp.jmp(elseBlockId))
	end 'emitOrderedBlockBody'

	// A copy of every branch edge of `blockRef`'s block.
	//
	// FRESH `BranchEdge` objects, not the same ones: the Target tier OWNS its BranchEdges and
	// REBUILDS them (`extractPredEdge` rebuilds the array to move an edge onto a split block, and
	// `rewriteEdgeArgsIn` swaps an edge's `argIds` to repoint it at a reload), so two blocks
	// sharing one edge object would let a later rewrite of one silently move the other. `argIds`
	// IS shared, and that is correct — nothing mutates it in place (see `prepareSkeleton`).
	function cloneBranchEdgesOf(blockRef BlockRef) returns BranchEdgeArray
		let block = try self.targetModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("cloneBranchEdgesOf: blockRef {blockRef} not in targetModule.blocks")
		var copied = BranchEdgeArray.create()
		for edge in block.branchEdges 'each'
			copied.push(BranchEdge.create(edge.targetBlockId, argIds: edge.argIds))
		end 'each'
		return copied
	end 'cloneBranchEdgesOf'

	// The id of `blockRef`'s block — a scoped read, so the caller never holds this borrow.
	function blockIdOf(blockRef BlockRef) returns BlockId
		let block = try self.targetModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("blockIdOf: blockRef {blockRef} not in targetModule.blocks")
		return block.id
	end 'blockIdOf'

	// The three fields of the `condBranch` terminating `blockRef`'s Std block, in one read and one
	// match — a TUPLE, because three one-field readers would be the same lookup and the same two
	// panic arms written three times, and a fourth field would have to be added to all of them.
	// (A tuple, not a payload-carrying union: `fuseIfDefines`'s heap-box warning is about unions,
	// and this runs once per SPLIT rather than once per op regardless.)
	function condBranchFieldsOf(blockRef BlockRef) returns (ValueId, BlockId, BlockId)
		let block = try self.stdModule.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("condBranchFieldsOf: blockRef {blockRef} not in stdModule.blocks")
		let term = try self.stdModule.ops.get(block.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("condBranchFieldsOf: terminatorIndex of block ref {blockRef} not in stdModule.ops")
		return match term 'term'
			condBranch(condId, thenBlockId, elseBlockId) gives (condId, thenBlockId, elseBlockId)
			const to mod panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
			branch to storeIndirect panic("condBranchFieldsOf: the terminator of block ref {blockRef} is not a condBranch — only `blockNeedsFloatEqualitySplit` selects the blocks this reads, and it proves one")
		end 'term'
	end 'condBranchFieldsOf'

	// Lower a Std unconditional `branch` to a single `jmp` terminator.
	function lowerBranch(targetBlockId BlockId, dstBlock IrBlock)
		self.targetModule.setTerminator(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.jmp(targetBlockId))
	end 'lowerBranch'

	// Value return: move the value into the primary-return register (R8), then emit the
	// `ret`. A VOID return (`StdOp.retVoid`) carries no value at all and goes straight to
	// `emitRet` — it does not come through here.
	function lowerReturn(retValId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock, returnType StdReturnType, asTerminator bool)
		self.emitPrimaryReturnMove(retValId, dstBlock: dstBlock, returnType: returnType)
		self.emitRet(dstBlock, asTerminator: asTerminator)
	end 'lowerReturn'

	// Void return: nothing moves to the return register, because the caller reads
	// nothing from it.
	//
	// The DECLARED return type must agree, and this is the guard that says so. A
	// `retVoid` in a VALUE-returning function would emit the `ret` with R8 never
	// written, handing the caller whatever the last op happened to leave there — the
	// silent miscompile a bare `return` in a value-returning function used to produce,
	// back when the parser chose the return op from the token after `return` instead of
	// from the declared type. The parser now rejects that source (E3013), so a
	// disagreement reaching here is a LOWERING bug, and it fails loudly. This is the
	// exact mirror of `emitPrimaryReturnMove`'s guard: between them, neither direction
	// of the value/void contract can be broken quietly.
	function lowerVoidReturn(dstBlock IrBlock, returnType StdReturnType, asTerminator bool)
		if not stdReturnTypeIsVoid(returnType) 'valueExpected'
			panic("lowerStdToX64: a `retVoid` in a value-returning function — the return register would never be written, and the caller would read garbage")
		end 'valueExpected'

		self.emitRet(dstBlock, asTerminator: asTerminator)
	end 'lowerVoidReturn'

	// The bare `ret`, shared by the value and void returns — the ONE thing they have in
	// common. Frame teardown (`epilogue`) is inserted separately by the prologue/epilogue
	// pass. `asTerminator` toggles setTerminator (block-final) vs appendOp (the rare
	// mid-block ret the Maxon tier can emit for a partially-terminated block).
	function emitRet(dstBlock IrBlock, asTerminator bool)
		if asTerminator 'asTerm'
			self.targetModule.setTerminator(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.ret)
		end 'asTerm' else 'asBody'
			self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.ret)
		end 'asBody'
	end 'emitRet'

	// Move the primary return value into R8 (Maxon's internal calling convention
	// returns in R8, NOT RAX). The float (XMM0) return path GROWS in at M3 when floats
	// land.
	//
	// A `void` StdReturnType reaching here means a VALUE return (`StdOp.ret`) inside a
	// function declared void. What ESTABLISHES that it cannot is the parser: a return
	// statement's op is chosen from the function's DECLARED return type, and a `return
	// <expr>` in a void function is rejected there as E3005 (Parser.parseReturnStatement)
	// — it is not merely assumed to be absent. Before that check existed this panic was
	// reachable from ordinary source (`function f(x int) return x end`), which is how a
	// compiler crash gets shipped behind a comment asserting it cannot happen.
	//
	// So a `void` here is now a LOWERING bug, not a user error, and it panics rather than
	// silently dropping the value. `lowerVoidReturn` guards the opposite direction.
	function emitPrimaryReturnMove(retValId ValueId, dstBlock IrBlock, returnType StdReturnType)
		if stdReturnTypeIsVoid(returnType) 'void'
			panic("lowerStdToX64: a value `ret` in a void-returning function — a void return lowers to `StdOp.retVoid`, which never reaches this path")
		end 'void'
		if stdReturnTypeIsFloat(returnType) 'float'
			panic("lowerStdToX64: float return values (XMM0) are not supported until M3 — the `return 42` slice is integer-only")
		end 'float'
		self.targetModule.appendOp(dstBlock, op: TargetOp.movRegReg(X64VReg.physical(X64Register.r8), srcReg: X64VReg.virtual(retValId)))
	end 'emitPrimaryReturnMove'
end 'StdToX64Lowering'

// ⭐ THE ONE DOOR from a Std type to an x64 memory-access width — the tier boundary's whole job for
// the memory band, and the reason `X64AccessWidth` is a narrow target-tier enum rather than a
// `StdType` passed straight through.
//
// `i1` is ONE byte: a bool occupies one byte of storage (`StdTypeInfo.storageBytes`), loads through
// `movzx` so it reads back as a clean 0/1, and stores through the byte form. `i64` is the full
// word. Those two are what the language can put in a global today.
//
// Every other type PANICS rather than picking a nearby width. `i8`/`u8` are byte-sized and would be
// correct here — they are excluded anyway, because nothing PRODUCES them: a ranged alias narrower
// than i64 needs the `as` cast (P1.9) to reach a global, and the day it does, its signedness
// decides `movzx` vs `movsx`, which is a DECISION and not a width to default into. That is the
// same trap `StdBinOpcode`'s two right-shifts exist for.
// ⭐ `f64` IS `word64`, AND THE WIDTH ENUM DID NOT GROW A CASE FOR IT. An f64 IS 64 bits, so
// `word64` is the honest answer — and which INSTRUCTION moves those 64 bits (`mov` or `movsd`) is
// already decided, at emit time, by the class of the register the value was coloured into
// (`emitWord64Load`/`emitWord64Store`). A `float64` width would be that same fact written a
// second time, in a second place, free to disagree with the first — this file's signature bug.
// The width says HOW MANY BITS; the register says WHICH FILE. Two questions, two answers.
//
// `f32` still panics: it is 4 bytes, so it is genuinely a width this enum does not have, and it
// would need `movss` — a real gap, not a spelling. Nothing produces one (Maxon's `float` is f64).
function x64AccessWidthFor(t StdType) returns X64AccessWidth
	match t 'w'
		i1 then return X64AccessWidth.byte
		i64 then return X64AccessWidth.word64
		f64 then return X64AccessWidth.word64
		i8 to i32 then panic("x64AccessWidthFor: no `.data` access form for {t.name} — a narrow integer global needs the `as` cast (P1.9), and its SIGNEDNESS then decides movzx vs movsx rather than defaulting to a width")
		u8 to u64 then panic("x64AccessWidthFor: no `.data` access form for {t.name} — an unsigned global needs the `as` cast (P1.9)")
		f32 then panic("x64AccessWidthFor: no `.data` access form for {t.name} — an f32 is 4 bytes and moves through `movss`, a width this enum does not carry; Maxon's `float` is f64, so nothing produces one")
	end 'w'
end 'x64AccessWidthFor'

// A memory-band offset as an x64 displacement. `ByteOffset` is unsigned and unbounded; a disp32 is
// signed and 32-bit, so the narrowing is CHECKED rather than cast away. Every offset today is
// `GlobalSlotOffset` (0), and the bound starts mattering at P1.1, where a struct field's offset
// rides this same field.
function memoryDisplacement(offset ByteOffset) returns Imm32
	if offset > i32.max 'tooFar'
		panic("memoryDisplacement: byte offset {offset} does not fit an x64 disp32 — an addressing mode reaching past 2GiB from its base needs a materialized index register")
	end 'tooFar'
	return offset as Imm32
end 'memoryDisplacement'
